---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/expert/polymer-chemistry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Polymers are chemistry's lego: <strong>small molecules linked into chains of tens of
    thousands</strong>, whose properties come from chain length, tangling and packing rather
    than exotic chemistry. Polyethylene is just petrochemical ethene repeated — and it is
    made at roughly 100 million tonnes a year. The organic chemistry syllabus is, from this
    vantage point, a tour of the monomer supply: functional groups name the reactive sites,
    nomenclature compresses the vocabulary, fuels and cracking make the feedstock, and the
    alkanes-to-carboxylic-acids ladder is the catalogue of handles a polymer chemist can
    hang chemistry on. Nature ran the same playbook first: DNA, proteins, cellulose and
    rubber are all polymers.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the polymer logic chain — from monomer to material" widget="equationStep" config={polyCfg} />

  <h2>Formulae and functional groups: the monomer alphabet</h2>
  <p>
    Functional groups (11.1) are the reactive handles that make organic chemistry a
    combinatorial language: –OH (hydroxyl, alcohols), C=C (the alkene double bond),
    –COOH (carboxyl), –COO– (ester), –CONH– (amide). Molecular formulae stop being
    arbitrary once you see them as connectivity plus handles: C₂H₄ is ethene (the polymer
    feedstock), C₂H₆O is ethanol (the handle changes everything). For polymer chemistry
    the decisive groups are the ones that can open or condense: a C=C double bond can
    <em>add</em> monomers forever (addition polymerisation), while two complementary
    groups — an acid and an alcohol, an acid and an amine — can <em>condense</em>, kicking
    out water at every link. Every plastic on a shop shelf descends from one of those two
    mechanisms, which is why the functional-group table is the most load-bearing page of
    the school organic chapter.
  </p>

  <h2>Naming: nomenclature as compression</h2>
  <p>
    IUPAC nomenclature (11.2) looks like pedantry and works like a file format: a name
    encodes the carbon skeleton (meth-, eth-, prop-), the handles (-ol, -ene, -oic acid)
    and their positions, so any structure can be rebuilt from its name alone — "polymer"
    itself is just the prefix poly- ("many") attached to the monomer: poly(ethene),
    poly(propene), poly(chloroethene) = PVC. The discipline pays off industrially:
    "polyethylene terephthalate" tells a chemist instantly that the monomers are ethylene
    glycol and terephthalic acid, joined by ester links — hence recyclable by reversing
    esterification, hence the PET recycling symbol's existence. Short names (nylon 6,6)
    compress further: two six-carbon monomers, diamine and diacid, alternating — the name
    is the recipe. Nomenclature failures are real failures: an unlabelled drum with a
    trade name is a hazard; one with an IUPAC name is a document.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "poly(ethene) means the polymer is made of ethene
    units, so the properties should be like a giant alkane gas." The repeat unit keeps
    the <em>connectivity</em> but not the state: dispersion forces that were negligible
    per monomer accumulate over thousands of units, so C₂H₄ is a gas (boiling near
    −104 °C) while its polymer is a solid used for hot-water pipes. Polymer properties
    are emergent over the chain length — which is the subject's central point, and the
    reason a plastic's behaviour cannot be inferred from its monomer's.
  </p>

  <h2>Fuels: where the monomers come from</h2>
  <p>
    The fuels topic (11.3) is secretly polymer chemistry's supply chain. Crude oil is
    fractionally distilled into fractions, and the light naphtha fraction is
    <strong>cracked</strong> — heated with steam or a zeolite catalyst — to break big
    alkanes into small alkenes: decane C₁₀H₂₂ becomes ethene C₂H₄ plus octane C₈H₁₈ (one
    representative split among many). Ethene from steam cracking is the petrochemical
    industry's largest product, feeding polyethylene, PVC (via chloroethene), polystyrene
    (via ethylbenzene) and PET's glycol. The arithmetic that matters: a barrel of crude
    is roughly half fuel by volume, but the chemical fraction carries disproportionate
    value — and the carbon locked in a plastic bottle is carbon <em>not</em> immediately
    burned, which reframes recycling as carbon bookkeeping, not just waste management.
    The same C=C reactivity that lets ethene polymerise makes it burn explosively in
    air; chemistry's most useful double bond is also its most flammable.
  </p>

  <h2>Alkanes: the inert backbone</h2>
  <p>
    Alkanes (11.4) — CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, all single σ bonds — are the inert skeleton of the field:
    strong C–C (348 kJ/mol) and C–H (412 kJ/mol) bonds, no polarisation, no handles, so
    they react only in combustion and (with catalysts or UV light) substitution. Their
    physical trend teaches the polymer property lesson in miniature: boiling point climbs
    smoothly with chain length because dispersion forces accumulate — methane is a gas at
    −162 °C, butane a gas at −1 °C, octane a liquid boiling at 126 °C, candle wax (C₂₀
    and up) a solid, and polyethylene — the same chain, thousands of units long — a tough
    solid melting near 130 °C. One mechanism, five states of matter. That accumulation of
    weak forces is exactly why polymers are tunable: chain length (molecular weight) is
    the manufacturer's primary control knob, and the difference between a wax and a
    structural plastic is just where the synthesis was stopped.
  </p>

  <h2>Alkenes: the double bond that builds the plastic age</h2>
  <p>
    The C=C double bond (11.5) is polymer chemistry's engine: σ bond plus exposed π bond,
    electron-rich, eager to add electrophiles — bromine water decolourises (the school
    test), and under pressure with a catalyst the same addition repeats indefinitely:
    ethene → polyethylene, one monomer after another opening its double bond to join the
    chain, no atoms wasted. The reaction's industrial scaling needed the
    <strong>Ziegler–Natta catalysts</strong> (1955, Nobel 1963): titanium chloride and an
    organoaluminium co-catalyst, which not only lowered the pressure needed but controlled
    the chain's stereochemistry — isotactic polypropylene (methyl groups all on one side)
    packs into crystals and makes mouldable, strong containers where the atactic random
    version is a tacky wax. One catalyst, one geometric preference, a thousandfold product
    difference: stereochemistry is a materials variable, not an academic footnote.
    Polyethylene itself splits by branching: high-density PE (straight chains, packed,
    rigid — milk bottles) versus low-density PE (branched, tangled, flexible — squeeze
    bags), differing only in how the polymerisation was run.
  </p>

  <h2>Alcohols: the handle that changes the polymer</h2>
  <p>
    Alcohols (11.6) — the –OH handle — show how one functional group rewrites a molecule's
    chemistry. Ethanol vs ethane: the –OH makes it hydrogen-bonding (ethanol boils at 78
    °C, ethane at −89 °C), water-miscible, and reactive toward oxidation (to ethanal,
    then ethanoic acid) and esterification. For polymers, the alcohol handle is the
    condensation partner: <strong>ethylene glycol</strong> (two –OH groups) plus
    terephthalic acid (two –COOH) builds PET, the polyester of drink bottles and fleece —
    each link formed by esterification, water split out, the reaction driven forward by
    removing that water. Poly(vinyl alcohol) — made not from a vinyl alcohol monomer
    (which doesn't exist, it tautomerises) but by hydrolysing poly(vinyl acetate) — is the
    water-soluble polymer of detergent pods, a reminder that polymer monomers sometimes
    exist only as polymerised. Fermentation's ethanol (11.6's biology overlap) now feeds a
    green route too: bio-ethanol dehydrated to bio-ethene closes a renewable loop to the
    same polyethylene.
  </p>

  <h2>Carboxylic acids and esters: the condensation economy</h2>
  <p>
    Carboxylic acids (11.7) –COOH bring the second handle of condensation chemistry:
    esterification with alcohols (acid catalysed, water out) and amide formation with
    amines. These are the reactions of nylon and PET — and of biology (ester links in
    fats, amide links in proteins — the biochemistry-bridge page picks this thread up).
    Nylon 6,6 (Carothers, DuPont, 1935 — the first fully synthetic fibre, announced 1938)
    condenses hexamethylenediamine with adipic acid: alternating amide links, hydrogen
    bonding between chains, a fibre that outperformed silk. Worked stoichiometry for a
    condensation: each amide link releases one water, so polymerising n diamine with n
    diacid liberates 2n − 1 (end-groups) water molecules — an 18 g/mol tax per link that
    must be removed or the reaction stalls at oligomer. The equilibrium is the design
    constraint: condensation polymerisation is reversible, which is precisely why
    polyesters hydrolyse slowly and why PET can be chemically recycled by pushing the
    reaction backwards.
  </p>

  <h2>Polymers: the two mechanisms and what they buy</h2>
  <p>
    The polymer topic (11.8) organises the field into two mechanisms and their property
    consequences. <strong>Addition</strong> (alkene openings): no by-product, chain grows
    at one end, polyethylene, PVC, polystyrene. <strong>Condensation</strong> (two
    complementary handles): by-product water, stepwise growth linking any two chains,
    nylons, polyesters, Kevlar. Properties then follow chain architecture:
    <em>molecular weight</em> (toughness — short chains are waxes), <em>crystallinity</em>
    (straight regular chains pack into ordered regions: stiff, opaque, stronger; tangled
    coils stay amorphous, clear, soft — cooling speed decides the mix), <em>cross-linking</em>
    (vulcanised rubber, Goodyear 1839: a few sulfur bridges turn sticky gum into a springy
    network that recovers instead of flowing), and the glass transition (below T_g chains
    freeze; a plastic aged below it is brittle, above it flexible). The honest ledger:
    ~9% of all plastic ever made has been recycled, because those inert σ-backbones
    resist everything including decay — the frontier is enzymes that digest PET
    (discovered in a Japanese compost heap, Ideonella sakaiensis, engineered since 2016)
    and polymers designed to depolymerise back to clean monomers on demand.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "polyethylene is a giant alkane molecule, so a
    polyethylene bag is one enormous molecule covalently bonded edge to edge." Partly
    right, hugely wrong in the implication: within one chain, yes — covalent bonds
    thousands of carbons long (molecular weights of 100,000+ are ordinary). But the
    <em>bag</em> is held together by intermolecular forces between chains: pull it apart
    and you slide or tear chains past each other; you rarely break the backbone. That is
    why polyethylene melts (intermolecular forces overcome) rather than decomposing into
    monomers, and why the melt can be remoulded — the thermoplastic behaviour
    thermosets (cross-linked, one giant network, Bakelite) deliberately give up.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "biodegradable and compostable plastics break down in
    landfill, so they solve the waste problem." Most "biodegradable" polymers (PLA, from
    lactic acid) need industrial composting conditions — 58 °C, controlled humidity,
    specific microbes — and essentially persist in cold, dry, oxygen-poor landfill; in
    the open ocean they behave much like conventional plastic on the timescales that
    matter. Degradation is a specified process with specified conditions, not a property
    the material carries everywhere. The chemistry is real; the marketing label outran it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Living polymerisation and precision architecture.</strong> Classical
    polymerisations die randomly (chain termination), giving broad molecular-weight
    spreads. Living/controlled polymerisations (anionic, 1956, Szwarc; later ATRP and
    RAFT) suppress termination, letting chains grow uniformly — and letting chemists
    stack blocks: A-block-B-block-C copolymers that self-assemble into nanometre-scale
    patterns (used as lithography templates), star and bottlebrush architectures, and
    single-chain nanoparticles. Molecular weight distributions that once spanned
    hundreds now routinely land within a few percent — polymers became a precision
    synthesis field, with sequence-defined polymers (information-carrying chains, the
    synthetic answer to proteins) as the active frontier.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Recycling, honestly costed.</strong> Mechanical recycling shreds and remelts
    but degrades chains each cycle (a PET bottle's second life is fibre, its third is
    filler — the "downcycling" staircase). Chemical recycling reverses the synthesis:
    PET glycolysis/methanolysis back to monomers (one Japanese plant has run this since
    the 1990s), enzymatic routes using engineered PETase reaching industrial pilots
    (Carbios, France), and depolymerisable "closed-loop" polymers (PDK and similar)
    designed from the start to un-zip on cue. The economics, not the chemistry, is the
    bottleneck: virgin petro-polymer is cheap because oil externalises its costs, which
    is why policy (deposit schemes, recycled-content mandates) moves the numbers more
    than catalysts do. The chemistry, though, is no longer the excuse.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Polymers at the frontier of performance.</strong> Kevlar (liquid-crystal
    spinning of rigid rod chains — stronger than steel by weight) and the aramids;
    conductive polymers (polyacetylene doped to metallic conductivity — Nobel 2000,
    Heeger, MacDiarmid, Shirakawa) founding the OLED and flexible-electronics industries;
    hydrogels that hold ninety-something percent water (contact lenses, tissue
    scaffolds); self-healing polymers with embedded reversible bonds. The design
    pattern is constant: pick the monomer handles (11.1), pick the mechanism (11.8),
    and the material follows. The syllabus's alkene-to-acid ladder is, read forward, a
    catalogue of the twenty-first century's materials supply chain.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Carothers (1935); Staudinger's macromolecule hypothesis (Nobel 1953); OECD Global Plastics Outlook.</p>
</TopicPage>
